</code></pre>

Q: How can I cut a string after X characters? A: <p>Use <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/String/substring" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>String.prototype.substring(<i>start</i>, <i>end</i>)</code></a>.</p>
<pre><code>let truncated = 'abcdef'.substring(0, 3) + '\u2026';
</code></pre>
<p>If you only want to add an ellipsis when the string is too long to begin with, check the string’s length first:</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const truncateText = (text, length) =&gt; {
  if (text.length &lt;= length) {
    return text;
  }

  return text.substring(0, length) + '\u2026';
};

let truncated;
truncated = truncateText('Hello, World!', 10);
console.log(truncated);
truncated = truncateText('Hello, World!', 50);
console.log(truncated);</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<p>Note that <code>substring</code>’s parameters are measured in UTF-16<a href="https://mathiasbynens.be/notes/javascript-encoding" rel="nofollow noreferrer" title="details on JavaScript string encoding">*</a> code units, so supporting Unicode graphemes that aren’t made up of single points in the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plane_(Unicode)#Basic_Multilingual_Plane" rel="nofollow noreferrer" title="Basic Multilingual Plane">BMP</a> requires extra care. A naïve <code>substring</code> on arbitrary text can leave dangling surrogates or partial composed characters.</p>
<pre><code>truncateText('😮‍💨', 2)
// '😮…'
</code></pre>
